gtY(){return"RIR"},
gtX(){return"How many more reps you could still do before failure. Helps track intensity."},
gtr(){return"1RM (one-rep max)"},
gtq(){return"Estimated maximum weight for one full repetition from your current weight and reps. A key strength benchmark for tracking progress and picking working weights."},
gt8(){return"Exercise volume (total)"},
gt7(){return"Next to the progress (chart) button under the sets: total tonnage for this exercise\u2014the sum of weight \xd7 reps for every set that has both values. Updates live as you add or edit sets."},
gp7(){return"Personal progress"},
gts(){return"One table of every movement from saved workouts: best set, estimated 1RM, and max volume. Search to find a name. Star favorites; tap the small chip under the name to tag a muscle group; the red X removes the name from suggestions and this table (saved workouts stay). Open it from your training menu or a client profile."},
guc(){return"Saving"},
gte(){return"Finish workout"},
gtd(){return"Confirms and writes the session to history. If you leave earlier, you can save, discard, or stay. A draft is also kept automatically if the app is interrupted."},
gu2(){return"Cardio & intensity"},
grT(){return"Cardio"},
grS(){return"Tracks time and intensity for cardio (distance is not entered). Helps you keep pace and monitor effort."},
gth(){return"Intensity"},
gtg(){return"Use the effort scale to gauge how hard the workout felt. That is the key to progress without overtraining."},
gue(){return"Stopwatch (your assistant)"},
gux(){return"Why use it?"},
guw(){return"To control rest between sets. Short rest \u2014 higher density; long rest \u2014 more strength for heavy weight."},
gum(){return"How does it work?"},
gul(){return"Center: time, Play/Pause, and Reset. Left and right: quick presets (below). In plain stopwatch mode time runs up with centiseconds. The timer stays pinned in the header while you scroll."},
guo(){return"Left: intervals"},
gun(){return"1/1 \u2014 60 s work, 60 s rest. 4/4 \u2014 4 minutes work and 4 minutes rest each round. 20/10 \u2014 Tabata-style: 20 s on, 10 s off. After you pick one, the timer loops work and rest until you pause or reset. Red tint means work; blue means rest."},
gut(){return"Right: rest countdown"},
gus(){return"+1m, +2m, and +5m start or extend a normal minute countdown. If an interval loop was running, it switches to this countdown. If a countdown is already running, the minutes are added to the time left. Handy for rest between sets."},
gur(){return"Progress bar"},
guq(){return"During countdown and interval modes, a bar under the time shows how much of the current phase remains."},
guv(){return"Sound & haptics"},
guu(){return"At 3, 2, and 1 seconds left in a phase, a short in-app sound and light haptic play. When the phase ends or work/rest switches, a stronger cue and haptic follow. Sounds are minimal bundled clips\u2014no microphone, no notification permission; media/system volume still affects loudness."},
gu8(){return"History & data"},
gtm(){return"History / statistics"},
gtl(){return"In calendar/history each workout shows total tonnage. Workout volume is one of the best indicators of hypertrophy: the more quality volume you accumulate, the more muscle you can build."},
gtp(){return"Import / export"},
gtn(){return"Back up or transfer your data using JSON export and import in the statistics dialog. Backups include workout history, your exercise list, favorites, and muscle-group tags. Coach backups are separate from personal history. A coach can also import an athlete's history as a new client without mixing exercise names."},
gu5(){return"Home"},
guB(){return"Theme"},
guA(){return"The sun / moon switch on the home screen toggles light and dark. Your choice is remembered."},
guG(){return"I train myself"},
guF(){return"Your own workouts, calendar, personal progress, favorites, a rhythm gauge of how often you train, and a full-body quick workout built from your tagged exercises."},
guE(){return"I train others"},
guD(){return"Coach mode: clients, a schedule, and a separate history per person. Each client has their own progress table and favorites."},
gu0(){return"Your training"},
guk(){return"Start workout"},
guj(){return"Opens an empty session \u2014 it does not copy the last workout. Add exercises, log sets, then finish to save."},
gtH(){return"Quick workout"},
gtE(){return"Assembles a 4-exercise full-body session: chest, back, one leg movement (front or rear thigh \u2014 never both), and one accessory for shoulders, arms, or core, whichever you have trained least recently. Prefers favorites. Last weights and reps appear greyed as a target to beat \u2014 you log each set yourself."},
grR(){return"Workout calendar"},
grQ(){return"See which days you trained, open a saved session, or add a workout on a chosen date and time \u2014 including past days."},
gtt(){return"A table of your best sets, estimated 1RM, and max volume. Star favorites and tag muscle groups here."},
gtc(){return"Favorite exercises"},
gtb(){return"Your starred list. Tick the ones you want and tap Build workout to start a session in that order. Last weights and reps appear greyed as a target to beat."},
gua(){return"Training rhythm"},
gtW(){return"After a few strength sessions, a gauge appears at the top of your training screen. It shows how many days per week you have trained on average over the last four weeks. Cardio-only days are not counted. The number is a snapshot of your density \u2014 not a target the app expects you to hit. Tap it for a note about your pace and what an active recovery week can look like."},
gtV(){return"Days per week"},
gtU(){return"The arc fills from 1 to 6 days per week. Color moves from steel through gold toward rust as density rises. The gauge appears once you have at least three strength days in that four-week window."},
gtT(){return"Active recovery week reminder"},
gtS(){return"If you have been training about two and a half days a week or more for several weeks without an active recovery week, the app may remind you that a short active recovery week is sometimes useful \u2014 about 30% less tonnage and reps. The more days per week, the sooner that reminder. It is a reminder, not a prescription. You will see it at most once per week, and only in your own training mode \u2014 not automatically for clients. Tap a client's gauge for the same note."},
gu4(){return"Favorites"},
gta(){return"Star"},
gt9(){return"In Personal Progress, tap the star next to an exercise to add or remove it from favorites. Favorites stay at the top of the table."},
grM(){return"Build workout"},
grL(){return"On the favorites screen, tick the exercises you want. The button appears when at least one is selected. Last weights and reps appear greyed as a target to beat \u2014 you log each set yourself. For a client, the same action starts a session for that person."},
gu7(){return"Muscle groups & quick workout"},
gtk(){return"Groups are optional labels you attach to your own exercise names. They are not a catalog: they only help Quick workout pick a balanced full-body mix. Eight groups: chest, back, shoulders, core, front thigh, rear thigh, arm curl, arm extension."},
gtj(){return"How to tag"},
gti(){return"When you first enter a new exercise name, the app asks for a group. You can skip with Later, or mark Not for quick workout if you do not want that movement in a full-body mix. Tap the small chip under the name in Personal Progress or Favorites to change or clear it."},
gtG(){return"How a quick session is built"},
gtF(){return"Four slots: chest, back, one half of the legs, then shoulders/arms/core by recency. A preview lets you swap any slot for another exercise in the same group. Add more yourself if you want a longer session."},
guz(){return"Strength / Cardio"},
guy(){return"Switch at the top of the session. Strength: weight, reps, RIR. Cardio: time and intensity only \u2014 no distance field."},
gtw(){return"Grey last-session numbers"},
